\subsection{From analysis to a candidate sea-state schedule}
\label{sec:riccati-similarity-design-guidance}

The reduced analyses support three scheduler principles without uniquely fixing
a law: $\sigma_{aw}\tau^3$ sets the third-integral scale, scaling pseudo-update
period with $\tau$ approximately preserves updates per OU correlation time, and
regularization trades drift suppression against signal distortion.  These
principles define the implementation family calibrated with the complete MEKF.

\subsubsection{Candidate wrapper law}
\label{sec:riccati-deployed-cadence}

The deployed wrapper forms the cubic base target
\begin{equation}
r_S^{\mathrm{base}}=c_R\sigma_{aw}\tau^3,
\end{equation}
and scales pseudo-update cadence with the applied OU time scale,
\begin{equation}
T_S=\clip(c_T\tau_{\mathrm{appl}},T_{S,\min},T_{S,\max}).
\end{equation}
To preserve the historical pseudo-measurement information rate as cadence
changes, the filter receives
\begin{equation}
r_{S,\mathrm{filter}}
=r_S^{\mathrm{base}}\sqrt{\frac{T_{S,0}}{T_S}}.
\label{eq:deployed-rs-summary}
\end{equation}
Away from clamps,
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
r_{S,\mathrm{filter}}\propto\sigma_{aw}\tau^{5/2}.}
\label{eq:deployed-effective-law}
\end{equation}
This engineering schedule is not the closed-form optimum of
Eq.~\eqref{eq:reduced-optimal-rs}, which depends on $m_{-4}$ and coupled
$q_{\mathrm{eff}}$ not available as independent online quantities.  The
implementation instead uses estimated wave time scale and acceleration level,
bounded smoothing, and a coefficient calibrated by full-filter experiments.

\subsubsection{Empirical amplitude choice}
\label{sec:riccati-law-ablation}

Paired exponent ablation finds that linear dependence on $\sigma_{aw}$ performs
best among the tested amplitude exponents.  This supports the deployed schedule
for the evaluated sea family but does not uniquely decompose low-frequency
acceleration error into sensor noise, attitude leakage, residual bias, and model
mismatch.

Section~\ref{sec:adaptation} specifies the deployed coefficients, bounds,
averaging horizons, and one-sample staging.  The separation is intentional:
theory identifies mechanisms and scaling variables; complete-estimator
experiments select and validate the engineering schedule.
